mod export os;

// DOCS -- `os` module.
//
// Platform identity for the unified core library. User Silver code asks
// which OS it is running on with the runtime `os.type` check, compared
// against the `OSTypes` enum below. OS code inside the std modules does not
// use this check -- those modules select their OS code at compile time with
// `#on WINDOWS` regions; this module is the user-facing runtime query.
//
// The native half lives in silverc-rs/src/stdlib/os.rs (registered in
// src/stdlib/mod.rs). `os.type`, `os.name` and `os.version` are emitted
// directly as x86-64 code: `os.type()` is the compile-time constant WINDOWS
// (this backend only emits Windows PE executables), `os.name()` is the static
// string "windows", and `os.version()` calls ntdll!RtlGetVersion and formats
// "major.minor.build".
//
// The `OSTypes` variant order is the API contract shared with the native
// constants (OS_TYPE_WINDOWS/LINUX/DARWIN/UNKNOWN in stdlib/os.rs); future
// backends must keep these values:
//     WINDOWS = 0, LINUX = 1, DARWIN = 2, UNKNOWN = 3
//
// MAC.md section 1b (naming rule, permanent): the preprocessor word is MAC
// (`#on MAC`), the CLI flag is `--os mac`, and only the `os.type` runtime
// query answers DARWIN -- the enum variant is DARWIN (never MACOS).
// Mirrors silverc-rs/std/os.sr on the enum/contract.

def OSTypes = enum {
    WINDOWS,
    LINUX,
    DARWIN,
    UNKNOWN
};

// Self-host/interpreter stand-in for the native `os.type` query, part of
// the module's public API. The silverc-rs backend answers `os.type()` calls
// natively (compile-time WINDOWS constant in stdlib/os.rs); the self-hosted
// compiler's builtin `os` dispatch only covers os.name/os.version/os.build,
// so this plain-Silver helper supplies the query for code compiled by the
// self-host and for the interpreter. It compiles everywhere Silver compiles
// and returns the same contract value: OSTypes.WINDOWS (0) on this
// Windows-only backend.
//
// SELF-HOST DIFFERENCE (why this helper exists here but not in
// silverc-rs/std/os.sr): the reference module is answered natively by the
// Rust backend and carries no Silver helper; the self-host needs this
// stand-in because its `os` module is builtin-reserved (its source is never
// collected, so mini_cg.sr mirrors the enum contract directly, DARWIN
// included). A per-target `#on MAC return OSTypes.DARWIN` arm stays
// hardware-gated with the rest of the Mac execution path.
public int osType() {
    return OSTypes.WINDOWS;
}
